  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "When the decimal point of a certain positive decimal number is moved four places to the right, the new number is four times the reciprocal of the original number. What is the original number?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(0.0002\\)",
    "B": "\\(0.002\\)",
    "C": "\\(0.02\\)",
    "D": "\\(0.2\\)",
    "E": "\\(2\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Let the original number be \\(x>0\\). Moving its decimal point four places right multiplies it by \\(10{,}000\\), while four times its reciprocal is \\(4/x\\). Hence\n\\[\n10{,}000x=\\frac4x.\n\\]\nSince \\(x>0\\), multiplying by \\(x\\) is valid. We obtain\n\\[\n10{,}000x^2=4,\n\\qquad (100x)^2=4.\n\\]\nThe square-root candidates are \\(x=\\pm2/100\\). Positivity leaves \\(x=0.02\\).\n\nChecking the original condition, shifting \\(0.02\\) gives \\(200\\), and four times its reciprocal is \\(4\\cdot50=200\\). The original number is \\(\\boxed{0.02}\\), choice C.",
